Hi, and thanks for your reply.

On 27 Mar, 16:36, Henrik Bengtsson <henrik.bengts...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> It is not clear from your description what the problem is.  What to do
> you mean by "skewed toward deletions"?  Do you mean there are a higher
> number of deleted regions, or do you mean that the CN mean levels are
> shifted down away from CN=2, or something else?
>

There are higher numbers of deletion calls.

My earlier post was about a pilot run of 50 normal male individuals in
which there were many more deletion calls than insertions
(Affy 500K Nsp+Sty called by GLAD, CNVs 5+ SNPs in length, having CN
>2.5 copies or <1.5 copies).
112 insertions
271 deletions

I was surprised by this bias.  Earlier studies, done on other
platforms (whole-genome tiling array, for example) had varying
results.  Multiple studies had similar numbers of insertions and
deletions.  However, this was not by any means the rule:

Redon et al, 2006 (whole-genome tiling array on 270 HapMap
individuals):
10834 insertions
7901 deletions

Redon et al, 2006 (Affy 500K SNP early access array on 270 HapMap
samples):
3004 insertions
3454 deletions

International Schizophrenia Consortium, 2008 (controls only, Affy 5, 6
arrays):
1873 duplications
1278 deletions

Kidd et al, 2008 (8 individuals, variety of methods including
sequencing and validation by custom high-density oligo arrays and
Illumina 1M arrays):
1258 insertions
1164 deletions

Conrad et al, 2009 (Nimblegen 42M oligo array on 40 HapMap
individuals):
1185 regions with insertions
4414 regions with deletions

Tam et al, 2010 (whole-genome tiling array on 92 normal individuals):
2038 insertions
2003 deletions

So, apparently the jury is still out on the insertion/deletion balance
in normal individuals.

Louie

